    "engine": "jupyter",
    "markdown": "---\ngd-head-tags:\n  - >-\n    `<script type=\"application/ld+json\">[{\"@context\": \"https://schema.org\",\n    \"@type\": \"SoftwareSourceCode\", \"name\": \"EdgeCase\", \"description\":\n    \"High-performance graph analysis library\", \"codeRepository\":\n    \"https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase\", \"license\":\n    \"https://opensource.org/licenses/MIT\", \"url\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase\", \"documentation\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase\", \"programmingLanguage\": {\"@type\":\n    \"ComputerLanguage\", \"name\": \"Python\"}}, {\"@context\": \"https://schema.org\",\n    \"@type\": \"WebPage\", \"name\": \"Graph.radius - EdgeCase\", \"isPartOf\": {\"@type\":\n    \"WebSite\", \"name\": \"EdgeCase\"}, \"url\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase/reference/Graph.radius.html\"}]</script>`{=html}\nopen-graph:\n  title: >-\n    [Graph.radius()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n    .doc-label-method} | EdgeCase\n  description: Get graph radius (minimum eccentricity).\ntwitter-card:\n  title: >-\n    [Graph.radius()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n    .doc-label-method} | EdgeCase\n  description: Get graph radius (minimum eccentricity).\ntitle: >-\n  [Graph.radius()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label .doc-label-method}\nbody-classes: doc-api-page doc-py-api-page\nshift-heading-level-by: 0\nbread-crumbs: false\ntoc-depth: 3\ndescription-meta: Get graph radius (minimum eccentricity).\npage-navigation: false\nhtml-table-processing: none\n---\n\n::: {.doc-subject}\n\nGet graph radius (minimum eccentricity).\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-usage-source}\nUsage\n\n[Source](https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase/blob/main/python/edgecase/graph.py#L66401-L66512){target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"}\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-signature .doc-function}\n```pythonsignature\nGraph.radius(unconnected=True)\n```\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-text}\n\n![Radius](../assets/graphs/radius.light.svg){.lightbox fig-alt=\"A six-node graph with each node's eccentricity in a badge. The center nodes C and D, drawn in the accent color, reach every other node within 2 hops, so the radius is 2.\" width=\"100%\"}\n\nThe radius is the minimum of all node eccentricities. Nodes with eccentricity equal to the\nradius form the center of the graph.\n\nA node's *eccentricity* is the distance to the farthest node it can reach. It is computed\nwith a breadth-first search from every node, so distances are counted in hops (edge\nweights are ignored) and, in directed graphs, paths must follow edge directions. The\nradius tells you how close the best-placed node is to everyone else, which is useful for:\n\n- choosing where to put a facility, server, or coordinator so that the worst-case\n  distance to anyone is as small as possible\n- comparing it with the diameter: a radius close to half the diameter indicates a\n  compact, well-centered network, while a radius close to the diameter indicates one\n  without a clear center\n- bounding the number of rounds a broadcast from the best starting node needs\n\nA node's eccentricity only covers the nodes it can reach, so a node that reaches nothing\n(an isolated node, or a sink in a directed graph) would have eccentricity `0`. To keep\nsuch nodes from dragging the radius to `0`, only nodes that reach every node of their\n(weakly) connected component are considered, and single-node components are skipped\nunless the graph has no edges between distinct nodes (then the radius is `0`). In an\nundirected graph every node reaches its component, so this is the smallest component\nradius among components with at least two nodes. In a directed graph a node counts only\nif every node of its component is reachable from it: a directed path `A -> B -> C` has\nradius `2` (only `A` reaches everything), and if no node reaches its whole component the\nresult is `None`. `center()` returns the nodes that attain it, and `diameter()` is\nits counterpart (the largest eccentricity).\n:::\n\n## Parameters {.doc-parameters}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code>[unconnected]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [bool]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [[True]{.va}]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>\n\n:   Controls what happens when some nodes cannot reach each other. If `True` (default),\n    the smallest eccentricity is taken across all components, skipping single-node\n    components (so an isolated node doesn't make the radius `0`). If `False`, the method\n    returns `None` unless every node can reach every other (strong connectivity for\n    directed graphs, as in `diameter()` and `mean_distance()`).\n:::\n\n## Returns {.doc-returns}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code> [int | None]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>\n\n:   Minimum eccentricity, or `None` if disconnected (when `unconnected=False`). Also\n    `None` for an empty graph, or a directed graph where no node reaches its whole\n    component.\n:::\n\n## See Also {.doc-see-also}\n\n[](`~diameter`) \n\n:   The largest eccentricity.\n\n[](`~center`) \n\n:   The nodes whose eccentricity equals the radius.\n\n[](`~eccentricity`) \n\n:   Each node's distance to the node farthest from it.\n\n## Examples {.doc-examples}\n\nIn a star, the hub reaches every other node in one hop, so the radius is `1`:\n\n::: {#c41079af .cell execution_count=1}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nimport edgecase as ec\n\ngraph = ec.Graph.from_edges([(\"hub\", \"A\"), (\"hub\", \"B\"), (\"hub\", \"C\")])\n\ngraph.radius()\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=1}\n```\n1\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\nIn a path of five nodes, the middle node is at most two hops from either end, so the\nradius is `2`, half the diameter of `4`:\n\n::: {#e963e152 .cell execution_count=2}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\npath = ec.Graph().add_path_graph(5)\npath.radius(), path.diameter()\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=2}\n```\n(2, 4)\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\nAn isolated node reaches nothing, so it is skipped and the star's radius of `1` stands.\nUse `unconnected=False` to get `None` for any disconnected graph instead:\n\n::: {#715216af .cell execution_count=3}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\ngraph.add_node(label=\"loner\")\ngraph.radius(), graph.radius(unconnected=False)\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=3}\n```\n(1, None)\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\nIn a directed graph only nodes that reach their whole component count. Along the path\n`A -> B -> C` that is just `A`, whose farthest node is two hops away:\n\n::: {#df548bb4 .cell execution_count=4}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nchain = ec.Graph.from_edges([(\"A\", \"B\"), (\"B\", \"C\")], directed=True)\nchain.radius()\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=4}\n```\n2\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\n### The center of the metro\n\n`metro()` is a made-up city's metro network. Its radius is four stops: from Central or\nRiverside, every station is at most four stops away. These two stations are the center,\nwhich `center()` returns:\n\n::: {#dae98cd6 .cell execution_count=5}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nmetro = ec.metro()\nmiddle = metro.center()\n\nmetro.radius(), [metro.get_label_from_id(n) for n in middle]\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=5}\n```\n(4, ['Central', 'Riverside'])\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\n::: {#53fc137e .cell execution_count=6}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nmetro.plot(layout=\"pos\", highlight=middle, label_by=\"label\")\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=6}\n```{=html}\n<div class=\"ec-theme\" style=\"display:contents\"><style class=\"ec-theme-vars\">:where(.ec-theme){--ect-3239c383:#fafafa;--ect-e5dfd507:#94a3b8;--ect-8a2de1d4:#374151;--ect-57425a7e:rgba(15,23,42,0.88);--ect-21ade003:#f1f5f9;--ect-61169ea5:#c2410c;--ect-63cc1385:#d4d4d8;--ect-879b7ebc:#949497;--ect-b9f8de6d:#872d08;--ect-f59ba8df:#a1a1aa}@media (prefers-color-scheme: dark){.ec-theme{--ect-3239c383:#0d1117;--ect-e5dfd507: